'''Cley Hill''' ({{gbmapping|ST838449}}) is a prominent hill to the west of [[Warminster]] in [[Wiltshire]], England. Its summit has a commanding view of the Wiltshire / Somerset county boundary, at {{Convert|244|m|ft|abbr=}} elevation.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.hill-bagging.co.uk/mountaindetails.php?qu=S&rf=18014|title=Cley Hill|website=Database of British and Irish Hills|access-date=2 December 2019}}</ref> The land is in [[Corsley]] parish and is owned by the [[National Trust]].

A {{convert|26.6|ha|adj=on}} area of [[chalk grassland]] at Cley Hill was [[SSSI notification|notified]] as a [[biological Site of Special Scientific Interest]] in 1975.<ref>{{cite web|title=Citation sheet for Cley Hill|url=https://designatedsites.naturalengland.org.uk/PDFsForWeb/Citation/1002573.pdf|publisher=Natural England|access-date=23 March 2022}}</ref> The land is managed by the National Trust, having been donated to the charity in 1954 by the [[Henry Thynne, 6th Marquess of Bath|6th Marquess of Bath]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Cley Hill|url=http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/cley-hill|publisher=National Trust|access-date=11 May 2016}}</ref>

Archaeological features include a large univallate [[British Iron Age|Iron Age]] [[hill fort]], two [[bowl barrow]]s and medieval [[strip lynchet]]s.<ref>{{National Heritage List for England|num=1017296|desc=Hillfort, two bowl barrows, medieval strip lynchets and a cross dyke on Cley Hill|access-date=11 May 2016}}</ref> In 1812,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Cley Hill Hillfort |url=https://www.themodernantiquarian.com/post/57977/miscellaneous/cley-hill |access-date=2025-05-15 |website=www.themodernantiquarian.com}}</ref> local [[antiquarian]] [[Sir Richard Hoare, 2nd Baronet|Sir Richard Hoare]] attempted to excavate the mound at the top of the hill – leaving a hole which is still visible – but found that it had been looted by [[Grave robbery|grave robbers]].<ref name=":0" />

The south west side of the hill was [[Quarry|quarried]] significantly in the 19th century.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Heritage Gateway - Results |url=https://www.heritagegateway.org.uk/Gateway/Results_Single.aspx?uid=207638&resourceID=19191 |access-date=2025-05-15 |website=www.heritagegateway.org.uk}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=martinpapworth |date=2013-09-28 |title=Upon Cley Hill |url=https://archaeologynationaltrustsw.wordpress.com/2013/09/28/upon-cley-hill/ |access-date=2025-05-15 |website=Archaeology National Trust SW |language=en}}</ref>

== Mythology ==
[[File:Cley Hill Digital Terrain Model.jpg|thumb|3D view of the digital terrain model]]{{See also|List of paranormal events reported in Wiltshire, England}}
There is a legend that the hill was formed by the devil, when he dropped a sack of earth with which he had planned to bury the town of [[Devizes]]. He had retrieved the earth from Somerset and was travelling to Devizes when he stopped to ask an old man the distance to the town. The man replied that he had been walking for years to reach Devizes, so the devil abandoned his plan.<ref>{{cite news|title=...while we bet this hill's a devil to climb|url=http://www.wiltshiretimes.co.uk/news/789155.while_we_bet_this_hills_a_devil_to_climb/|newspaper=[[Wiltshire Times]]|date=9 June 2006|access-date=5 August 2013}}</ref>

In 1924, V.S. Manley wrote in his book, ''Folklore of the Warminster District: a supplement to the History of Warminster and the Official Guide'':<ref>{{Cite web |last=eheard |date=2023-05-28 |title=Cley Hill - Elementals, UFOs and the Devil |url=https://weird-wiltshire.co.uk/2023/05/28/cley-hill-elementals-ufos-and-the-devil/ |access-date=2025-05-15 |website=Weird Wiltshire |language=en-US}}</ref>

{{Blockquote|text=One of the most curious items is 'The Spirit of Cley Hill', a legend which would apparently have died with its narrator, an old woman of 80. The legend records that the guardian spirit of the Bugley folk lived inside the barrow on the top of the hill, and one day hearing water running beneath him he directed its course underground until it came out at Hogs Well. He told the people not to drink it but to use it only for curing weak eyes, and an old woman who disregarded his order and drank the water died that night, and a cow that polluted the water was drowned in the mud. It is in any case a fact that until recently this water has been in great request for bad eyes, 6d. a bottle being paid for it, provided some Ground Ivy was included to be brewed with it. The appearance of the Well Fiend is recorded of Bicker’s Well, in Prince Croft Lane, at Bugley, and under a large oak tree which formerly stood where North Lane meets the Half, below Blue Ball, Bugley, elves lived and might sometimes be seen gambolling by children.}}

Since the 1965 sighting of the "[[Warminster#UFO sightings|Warminster thing]]", Cley Hill has been a hotspot of [[UFO sightings]] and [[Crop circle|crop circles]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Baker |first=Max |date=2019-08-17 |title=6 times UFOs have been spotted at local 'alien hotspot' |url=https://www.somersetlive.co.uk/news/somerset-news/ufo-cley-hill-warminster-frome-3209653 |access-date=2025-05-15 |website=Somerset Live |language=en}}</ref> Sightings were documented in 1967, 1996, 2001, 2005<ref>{{Cite web |last=Doyle |first=Dave |date=2022-02-27 |title=Stonehenge crop circles among most notorious UFO sightings |url=https://www.wiltshirelive.co.uk/news/wiltshire-news/wiltshires-most-notorious-ufo-sightings-6723205 |access-date=2025-05-15 |website=Wiltshire Live |language=en}}</ref> and 2017.<ref name=":1" />

